# --- Database (REQUIRED) ---
#
# This app runs on Postgres. There is no SQLite fallback any more: the Prisma
# client is generated from a `postgresql` schema, and Prisma binds the generated
# client to its provider, so one build serves one provider. A `file:` URL here
# now fails at startup with an explanation rather than half-working.
#
# Local development — a throwaway Postgres, one command:
#   docker run --rm -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres postgres:17
#   npm run db:setup      # migrate + seed
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/postgres?schema=public"
#
# Against the hosted database (Supabase): copy the string from
# Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string. THREE exist and they are
# not interchangeable:
#
#   - direct,             db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432
#       IPv6-only on the free tier. Unreachable from most home and mobile ISPs,
#       which are IPv4-only. Fails as "Can't reach database server", which does
#       not sound like an address-family problem and wastes an evening.
#   - session pooler,     <region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432
#       Use this for migrations. IPv4-reachable, holds a real session, so DDL
#       works. Note the username is postgres.<ref>, not postgres.
#   - transaction pooler, <region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543
#       The deployed app, with ?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1. Every
#       serverless invocation opens its own connection; anything unpooled
#       exhausts Postgres' connection limit under exactly the traffic you want.
#
# Pointing this at the hosted database is normal and safe for reading. The two
# commands that would destroy it — `db:seed`, which prunes, and `db:reset`,
# which drops the schema — refuse against any non-localhost host and tell you
# the one-line opt-in if you truly mean it. See src/server/db/destructive-guard.ts.

# --- TEST_DATABASE_URL (optional) ---
#
# The Postgres the integration suites run against. Each suite creates its own
# uniquely-named schema, applies the migrations into it, and drops it afterwards,
# so nothing already in the database is read or written. Unset, the suites fall
# back to DATABASE_URL — which is why that isolation matters.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/postgres"

# --- Supabase Auth (client) ---
#
# Both values are public identifiers, not secrets, and both ship to every
# visitor in the client bundle by design. Copy them from
# Supabase -> Project Settings -> API.
#
# The URL names the project. The key is a JWT asserting the `anon` role and
# nothing more; what protects your data is Row Level Security, not the secrecy
# of this string. Supabase now issues keys in two forms and either works here:
# the legacy `eyJ...` anon key, or the newer `sb_publishable_...` key.
#
# Leave both empty and the site still works. Sign-in reports itself as
# unavailable, `/account` and `/admin` are unreachable, and the catalogue,
# search, the branch locator and the whole order flow are unaffected — that is
# a supported configuration, not a broken one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

# The origin OAuth returns to, e.g. https://soko.example. Used to build the
# `/auth/callback` redirect that Google sends the visitor back through.
#
# Optional but recommended. Unset, the server derives it from the forwarded
# host, which is client-supplied — Supabase refuses any redirect not in the
# project's own allowlist so this is not exploitable, but deriving a redirect
# target from a request header is a habit worth not forming.
#
# Whatever you set here, the same origin's `/auth/callback` must also be listed
# under Supabase -> Authentication -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs, or
# every sign-in fails at the last step.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=""

# --- Admin access (server) ---
#
# Comma-separated email addresses that get admin rights immediately, before any
# `app_metadata` flag is set. This is the bootstrap: nothing inside the app can
# grant the first admin, and nothing should be able to — an app that can promote
# its own users is one bug away from letting a stranger do it.
#
# Read per request, so removing an address here locks that person out on their
# next page load. That immediacy is what makes it the right tool in a hurry;
# clearing an `app_metadata` flag does not end a token already issued.
SOKO_ADMIN_EMAILS=""

# The hostname the admin console lives on, e.g. soko-admin.vercel.app. Set it
# and /admin stops existing on the public site: typing the URL there returns the
# same 404 as a misspelled brand slug, because on that host the route genuinely
# is not served. Opening the admin hostname's root redirects to /admin, its
# robots.txt says Disallow, and its sitemap is gone.
#
# Leave it empty and nothing changes — /admin answers on whatever host is
# asking, exactly as before. That direction is deliberate: failing closed on a
# blank value would lock you out of the console with no way back in except a
# redeploy. Obscurity was never the boundary; SOKO_ADMIN_EMAILS and the
# app_metadata flag are, and they are unaffected by this setting.
#
# Two things must be true for the hostname to work:
#   1. It is added as a domain on the same Vercel project, so it serves this app.
#   2. Its `https://<host>/auth/callback` is listed under
#      Supabase -> Authentication -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs.
#      Without this, signing in on the admin site fails at the last step.
#
# Sessions are per-hostname, so signing in here is separate from signing in on
# the shop. That is a feature: closing the shop's session does not close this one.
SOKO_ADMIN_HOST=""

# --- Notifications (optional) ---
#
# Without any of these Soko sends no email at all and behaves exactly as before.
# That is the correct state for local development, CI and preview deployments —
# none of them should be emailing anybody.
#
# Set ONE of the two provider keys. With it, Soko emails itself when a business
# applies to be listed and when a customer sends an order request — the two
# events that otherwise sit in the database until somebody thinks to look.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY: from resend.com. Free tier 3,000 emails/month. Sends from
# onboarding@resend.dev with no domain set up, but only to the address that owns
# the account — which is exactly what these notifications do.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# BREVO_API_KEY: from brevo.com, the fallback. Free tier 300 emails/day. Brevo
# has no shared sender, so a Brevo deployment must also set NOTIFY_FROM to an
# address verified in the Brevo dashboard.
#
# It exists because resend.com itself was unreachable from Tanzania on
# 2026-09-01 — that domain resolves to 76.76.21.22 and that one address did not
# route from the owner's network, while api.resend.com, github.com and Soko's
# own deployment all did. Only the signup page has to open in your browser; the
# API is called from Vercel.
BREVO_API_KEY=""

# Where notifications go. Defaults to the address published on /contact
# (see src/lib/contact.ts), so leaving this empty is fine and normal.
SOKO_NOTIFY_EMAIL=""

# Who they come from, as "Name <address>" or a bare address. Required for Brevo
# (it must be a sender you have verified there). Optional for Resend until you
# verify a domain. RESEND_FROM is still read as the older name for this.
NOTIFY_FROM=""

# --- What is deliberately NOT here ---
#
# There is no service-account key and no server-side secret for authentication
# at all. The Firebase build needed one — a private key granting full project
# access that bypassed both Security Rules and App Check. Supabase Auth needs
# nothing of the kind for what this app does.
#
# In particular the Supabase **service_role** key must never appear in this
# file, in this repo, or in the deployment. It bypasses Row Level Security
# completely. Soko does not need it: Prisma reaches Postgres as the `postgres`
# role over DATABASE_URL, and Supabase is used for identity only.
